\section*{Exponents and Powers}

8CBMA12

\section*{12.1 Powers with Negative Exponents}

We know that \(10^{2}=100\) and \(10^{1}=10\). Continuing the pattern,

\[
10^{0}=1, \quad 10^{-1}=\frac{1}{10}, \quad 10^{-2}=\frac{1}{100}
\]

In general, for any non-zero integer \(a\),

\[
a^{-m}=\frac{1}{a^{m}}
\]

where \(m\) is a positive integer. \(a^{-m}\) is the multiplicative inverse of
\(a^{m}\).

Example 1: Find the value of \(3^{-4}\).

\[
3^{-4}=\frac{1}{3^{4}}=\frac{1}{81}
\]

\section*{12.2 Laws of Exponents}

For non-zero integers \(a\) and \(b\), and integers \(m\) and \(n\):

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline Law & Example \\
\hline\(a^{m} \times a^{n}=a^{m+n}\) & \(2^{3} \times 2^{-5}=2^{-2}\) \\
\hline\(\frac{a^{m}}{a^{n}}=a^{m-n}\) & \(\frac{5^{4}}{5^{6}}=5^{-2}\) \\
\hline\(\left(a^{m}\right)^{n}=a^{m n}\) & \(\left(3^{2}\right)^{-2}=3^{-4}\) \\
\hline\(a^{m} \times b^{m}=(a b)^{m}\) & \(2^{3} \times 5^{3}=10^{3}\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

<mathml><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>a</mn><mn>m</mn></msup></mfrac></mathml>

Example 2: Simplify \(\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{-3}\).

\[
\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{-3}=\frac{2^{-3}}{3^{-3}}=\frac{3^{3}}{2^{3}}=\frac{27}{8}
\]

\section*{12.3 Standard Form of Small Numbers}

The thickness of a sheet of paper is about \(0.0001 \mathrm{~m}\). In standard
form we write

\[
0.0001=1 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~m}
\]

Example 3: The size of a red blood cell is \(0.000007 \mathrm{~m}\). Express
it in standard form.

\[
0.000007=7 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{~m}
\]

\section*{Exercise 12.1}

1. Evaluate:
(i) \(5^{-2}\)
(ii) \((-2)^{-3}\)
(iii) \(\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^{-2}\)
2. Simplify and express the result as a power of 2: \(2^{5} \times 2^{-7} \times 2^{4}\).
3. Express the following in standard form:
(i) 0.0000034
(ii) 0.00098
4. The mass of a dust particle is \(7.68 \times 10^{-10} \mathrm{~kg}\). What
